WEBVTT
1
00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:06.660
In this lesson, we'll introduce lane
chain expression language, or LCEL.

2
00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:10.089
This is a new syntax that makes it much
easier, and more transparent to construct,

3
00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:15.119
Let's get to it.

4
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.460
A lot of the power of
Langchain comes from composing

5
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.579
And we have a new way of doing this,

6
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.239
and this is LCEL and a runnable
protocol which define a few things.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.579
First, it defines an
allowed set of input types,

8
00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:37.399
There's then a bunch of standard
methods, and we'll go over all of these,

9
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.179
There are ways of
modifying parameters at runtime,

10
00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:02.060
What is this interface that we
expect all runnables to expose?

11
00:01:08.019 --> 00:01:08.189
First, there are a few
common methods there's

12
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.430
There's invoke, which calls the
runnable on a single input. There's stream,

13
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:13.480
which calls it on a single
input in stream's backer response.

14
00:01:14.359 --> 00:01:14.609
And then, there's batch which
calls it on a list of inputs.

15
00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:22.480
For all of these synchronous methods,
there's also a corresponding async method.

16
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.459
There's common properties
that all runnables have,

17
00:01:29.019 --> 00:01:31.420
namely an input schema
and an output schema.

18
00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:36.739
And you can see a list of the input and
output types for common components below.

19
00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:39.459
Why use LCL?

20
00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:42.480
There are a few
benefits that this gets you.

21
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:47.340
First, you get async batch and
streaming support out of the box.

22
00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:01.780
Second, a big part of
working with LLMs is that

23
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.299
they're a little bit
unpredictable sometimes.

24
00:02:03.519 --> 00:02:05.879
They don't always respond
how you want them to respond.

25
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:06.980
And so, you can easily attach fallbacks
not only to LLMs but also to entire chains.

26
00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.900
Third, parallelism. LLM
calls can be time-consuming.

27
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.539
They often take a while
and so it's really important

28
00:02:20.539 --> 00:02:22.500
to run them in parallel if you can.

29
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.939
And the LCEL syntax
makes it easy to do that.

30
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.759
And fourth, logging is built in.

31
00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:32.009
As these chains and agents
get more and more complex,

32
00:02:32.009 --> 00:02:35.389
being able to see the
sequence of steps and the inputs and

33
00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:37.639
outputs becomes crucial for
building an LLM application.

34
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.300
And we've added things into the
open source library. And then,

35
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.539
And LCAL natively logs
all of these things,

36
00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.099
With that, it's time to get to the code
and see what this actually looks like.

37
00:02:56.439 --> 00:02:58.699
First, let's set up our
environment as before.

38
00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:03.000
We'll then import a few different
components that we're going to compose together.

39
00:03:09.479 --> 00:03:11.659
First, we'll import a prompt template.

40
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.550
Next, we'll import a language
model, and we'll be using OpenAI.

41
00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:30.379
And finally, we'll
import an output parser,

42
00:03:38.169 --> 00:03:40.550
Let's now use these
components to create a simple chain

43
00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:44.150
of prompt template to
language model to output parser.

44
00:03:44.909 --> 00:03:45.139
So first, let's create a prompt template.

45
00:03:58.419 --> 00:04:01.240
And we'll create one that asks the
language model to tell us a short joke.

46
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:13.229
We'll then initialize the language
model that we're going to use and

47
00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:15.810
finally we'll create the output
parser that we're going to use.

48
00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.620
And we can now call this chain
using the invoke method on some inputs.

49
00:04:31.939 --> 00:04:34.899
And the inputs here are going to be
the inputs to the prompt template. So,

50
00:04:42.939 --> 00:04:43.000
And so, if we call this, we
can see we get back a joke.

51
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:47.660
Why don't bears ever
get caught in traffic?

52
00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.120
Because they always take
the beariest best routes.

53
00:04:51.759 --> 00:04:54.079
Now's a good time to pause a little bit,

54
00:04:54.079 --> 00:04:57.430
pass in some other arguments to this
function, maybe change up the prompt,

55
00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.199
play around with it, and get a
sense for these different components,

56
00:05:03.959 --> 00:05:05.759
We'll now create a
slightly more complex chain.

57
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.579
In the previous short course, we went
over how to do retrieval augmented generation.

58
00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:11.360
And so, we'll replicate that same
process using link chain expression language.

59
00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:18.120
First, we need to set up our retriever.

60
00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.720
We're first going to create a
really simple vector store.

61
00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:24.009
We're going to do this by
initializing it with two texts. First,

62
00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:24.889
Harrison worked at Kensho. And next,
bears like to eat honey well. Then,

63
00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:00.000
If we call it on something different, if
we call it on what do bears like to eat,

64
00:06:00.399 --> 00:06:03.199
we can see that the first result that
we get back is bears like to eat honey.

65
00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.910
In order to create this pipeline,

66
00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:25.139
we're first going to create a
prompt that basically asks the language

67
00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:28.100
model to answer the question
based only on the following context.

68
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.980
And it has two variables here.
It has context and question.

69
00:06:31.699 --> 00:06:31.870
And so, we're going to
be passing in two things.

70
00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:36.860
And then, we're going to
be passing in the context.

71
00:06:48.439 --> 00:06:48.959
We want the first and only input to the
chain to be the user question. From then,

72
00:06:48.959 --> 00:06:50.120
we want to fetch relevant context. And
then, we want to pass that into the prompt.

73
00:07:10.379 --> 00:07:11.920
To do that, we're going to use a
runnable map which we can import this way.

74
00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.100
We want this runnable
map to have two elements.

75
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.009
We can write a lambda function that
calls retriever.getRelevantDocuments

76
00:07:44.009 --> 00:07:46.519
and passes in the question parameter.

77
00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.620
We also want to pass along the
original question parameter,

78
00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:57.800
From here, we want to pass
it into the prompt. And then,

79
00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:12.360
We can then name this as our
chain, and we now have a new runnable.

80
00:08:22.269 --> 00:08:22.500
And now, if we invoke this with a
question, like where did Harrison work,

81
00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:32.330
If we want to take a closer look at
what exactly is going on under the scenes,

82
00:08:33.049 --> 00:08:35.429
we can just look at this
runnable map directly.

83
00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:40.799
And then, if we just run this
on the same thing. And then,

84
00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:57.039
This is then getting passed into the
prompt which is then creating a prompt

85
00:09:06.129 --> 00:09:08.039
One of the other
things that we can do with

86
00:09:08.039 --> 00:09:09.570
runnables is we can
bind parameters to them.

87
00:09:22.299 --> 00:09:22.500
And so normally, this would look
like a prompt. And then, a model.

88
00:09:26.679 --> 00:09:28.320
But where do the functions go in? And so,

89
00:09:35.299 --> 00:09:38.850
And what this will do under the
hood is that when the model is called

90
00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.460
it will pass any parameters in
bind along to the invocation.

91
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:42.899
So now, if we create a runnable by
doing prompt and then pipe model,

92
00:10:13.759 --> 00:10:16.139
If you wanted to change that
you would just overwrite it.

93
00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:51.779
And now, we have these two
different functions here.

94
00:11:01.909 --> 00:11:04.490
We can just update the model by
binding it with new functions.

95
00:11:21.759 --> 00:11:21.970
And then, when we call this new
runnable which we're using the new model in,

96
00:11:31.409 --> 00:11:34.419
One of the really powerful
things about language and expression

97
00:11:34.419 --> 00:11:36.519
language is you can
easily attach fallbacks,

98
00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:39.230
not only to individual
components, but to entire sequences.

99
00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:48.309
In order to create a situation
where this is going to fail,

100
00:11:48.309 --> 00:11:50.639
we'll actually use an
older version of a language

101
00:11:50.639 --> 00:11:52.710
model from OpenAI that
isn't quite as good.

102
00:11:59.019 --> 00:12:03.360
To do that, we're going to import an
OpenAI language model from lanechain.llms.

103
00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:05.559
This is different than the chat models.

104
00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:09.610
The chat models are newer
types of models and so they're

105
00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:12.419
generally pretty good at
things like outputting JSON,

106
00:12:12.419 --> 00:12:13.129
but older models which is
lanechain.llms, generally aren't.

107
00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:25.649
We'll now create a simple
model and a simple chain.

108
00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.629
In this simple model we're going to be
setting temperature equals zero. So,

109
00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:41.210
Our chain is just going to
be calling this language

110
00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:43.409
model and then piping the
result into JSON.loads.

111
00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:47.850
This will break if the result of
the language model isn't valid JSON.

112
00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:51.110
Let's now create a challenge
problem statement for the language model.

113
00:12:51.309 --> 00:12:54.100
We're going to ask it to
write three poems in a JSON

114
00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.669
blob where each poem is
a JSON blob of a title,

115
00:12:56.669 --> 00:12:57.830
author, and the first line.

116
00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:01.330
This is a pretty challenging problem
and we want it to respond in valid JSON.

117
00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:02.269
And so, if we run the
simple model on this,

118
00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:19.120
But if we take this and we
try to decode it with JSON,

119
00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:23.509
we can see that we get a JSON
decode error because of what

120
00:13:23.509 --> 00:13:26.220
that model is outputting
isn't actually valid JSON.

121
00:13:27.779 --> 00:13:31.620
The new models from OpenAI are
pretty good at outputting valid JSON.

122
00:13:43.799 --> 00:13:44.039
And then, we're going to pipe
that string into JSON.loads.

123
00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:51.120
If we use the same
challenge with this model,

124
00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:53.659
we can see that it
correctly outsplits valid JSON.

125
00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:58.740
In order to use fallbacks, what we
can do is we can create a final chain,

126
00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:01.159
and we can start with the
simple chain. And then,

127
00:14:07.799 --> 00:14:11.419
In with fallbacks we'll pass
in a list of other runnables,

128
00:14:17.059 --> 00:14:17.980
If an error is raised, then it
goes through this list in order.

129
00:14:32.059 --> 00:14:34.299
If we now call this
chain on the challenge,

130
00:14:34.299 --> 00:14:36.779
we can see that it
responds in the correct format.

131
00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:53.080
Finally, let's talk about the
interface that these runnables expose.

132
00:14:53.639 --> 00:14:55.039
Let's go back to the
chain that we had before.

133
00:14:55.759 --> 00:14:57.000
We're asking it to tell us a joke.

134
00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:00.500
And let's take a look at a few
different elements of this interface.

135
00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.220
The one that we were using
from the get-go is invoke.

136
00:15:04.799 --> 00:15:08.159
This is a synchronous method
that calls it on one input.

137
00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:12.659
There's also batch which is going
to call it on a list of inputs.

138
00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:13.779
So here, we pass in two
different inputs, one with

139
00:15:20.179 --> 00:15:24.340
And under the hood this is actually
executing them in parallel as much as possible.

140
00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:27.940
We can also stream back responses.

141
00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:52.720
And finally, all of these methods
have corresponding asynchronous methods.

142
00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:10.450
That is it for this lesson,

143
00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:15.009
but there's a lot of different ways
that you can combine all these elements

144
00:16:15.009 --> 00:16:18.259
and put together really
complicated types of chains and sequences.

145
00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:24.080
We sometimes see sequences that get
up to 300 or 400 components in a row.

146
00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:25.019
And so, before moving on,

147
00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:33.080
Can you do two, three, even four
language model calls in a row?

148
00:16:33.279 --> 00:16:33.919
What does that look like?

149
00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:35.919
Lots of fun things to explore.

150
00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:39.779
In the next lesson, we're going to
combine what we just learned here,

151
00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:42.259
language and expression
language, with what we covered

152
00:16:42.259 --> 00:16:44.379
in the previous lesson,
open AI function calls.

153
00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:47.120
And we're going to show how to
use those two things together.